course: dictionary
lesson: 722
title: Barriers: things that stop us
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A barrier is a fence, gate, wall or bar that stops people or things from going somewhere. Its job is to keep you safe, or to keep you out of a place where it is not safe to be.
explain: «At a firework display, stand behind the barrier, because fireworks can burn and injure people who are too close.» The barrier is there to keep you at a safe distance, so you do not get hurt.
explain: A barrier can also be something that makes a thing hard to do. «A language barrier» means people who speak different languages find it hard to understand each other. The word is used in the same way, even though there is no fence at all.
fact: A barrier is a fence, gate, wall or bar that stops people or things from going somewhere.
fact: At a level crossing, the barriers come down when a train is coming, so you must stop and wait.
fact: Never climb over or go under a barrier, even if it looks easy to do.
fact: A «language barrier» is anything that makes talking hard when people speak different languages.
fact: Removing barriers helps everyone join in, because people can take part when the things stopping them are taken away.
exercise: At a level crossing, the barriers come down. Why? | Because the sun is too bright | *Because a train is coming | Because the road is being painted | Because it is time for lunch
exercise: «Stand behind the barrier at a firework display.» Why must you do this? | Because fireworks are very quiet | *Because fireworks can burn people who are too close | Because the barrier is made of glass | Because the barrier is a good place to sit
exercise: «A man climbed over a barrier at a zoo.» Find and copy the word that names the thing he climbed over. | *barrier | big | zoo | climbed
test: «A language barrier» means what? | A sunny day at the beach | *Something that makes talking hard when people speak different languages | A bell that rings loudly | A wall of books in a library
test: «Removing barriers helps everyone join in.» What does this suggest? | Shutting the school gate | Building a higher fence | Moving the lunch hall to a new place | *Taking away the things that stop people taking part
exam: «A fast train can need over a kilometre to stop, so level crossings have barriers.» Why do level crossings have barriers? | To tell the time | *To keep people and cars safe from a train that cannot stop quickly | To make the road look nicer | To give birds somewhere to rest
